/*
    ============================================================================
    27. THE ROLE LIBRARY, MEASURED AGAINST OUR DICTIONARY
    ============================================================================
    Market Intelligence (Chloe O'Donnell) maintains a library of 315 job titles
    with descriptions, sectors and role types. It was written specifically
    because this enrichment reads ORCID free text, so it is deliberately fuller
    than the 21-option list used in their surveys.

    60 of our 151 anchors appear in it, which is an independent confirmation of
    those - the first outside check our anchor list has had. The 238 titles
    below do NOT appear in our anchor list.

    NOTE ON ENCODING. The source file is CP1252, not UTF-8. Read as UTF-8 it
    silently mangles every accented title: `maitre de conferences` arrives as
    `ma tre de conf rences` and then matches nothing, which would have quietly
    dropped the most interesting non-English candidate in the file.

    A GAP IS NOT AUTOMATICALLY AN ANCHOR. The question this query answers is
    which of these gaps are real in ORCID: a title nobody types is not worth an
    anchor, and a title already landing in the right group does not need one.

    Read the output in three bands:
      frequency = 0        the library lists it, ORCID does not use it. Ignore.
      assigned correctly   already handled by a neighbouring anchor. Ignore.
      assigned wrongly, or unassigned, with real frequency   <- the candidates.

    And adding an anchor is not free. Measured history: `pathologist` pulled
    every science ending in -ologist, three separate times; `chief pharmacist`
    pulled `chief technology officer`, 1,841 records. Six of eight predictions
    about which anchor would misbehave were wrong. Add in a batch, then re-run
    the per-anchor breakdown and back out the magnets.
*/

select
    lib.title                         as library_title,
    lib.sector,
    lib.role_type,
    coalesce(d.frequency, 0)          as orcid_frequency,
    d.title_group                     as currently_assigned,
    round(d.similarity, 3)            as similarity,
    d.runner_up,
    d.decision_source,
    d.matched_anchors[safe_offset(0)] as nearest_anchor
